WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.925
How does injection moulding work?

2
00:00:03.475 --> 00:00:06.798
This is an injection moulding press, cut open.

3
00:00:07.348 --> 00:00:11.065
On the left the injection unit, on the right the mould.

4
00:00:11.965 --> 00:00:14.798
Pellets drop from the hopper into a heated barrel.

5
00:00:15.348 --> 00:00:18.564
Inside it a screw turns and carries them forward.

6
00:00:19.114 --> 00:00:21.111
The heat and the kneading melt them.

7
00:00:22.011 --> 00:00:26.960
Once there is enough melt in front of the screw, it shoots forward like a piston.

8
00:00:27.509 --> 00:00:29.808
That injects the melt into the mould.

9
00:00:30.708 --> 00:00:33.587
The cavity in the mould has the shape of the part.

10
00:00:34.137 --> 00:00:37.817
Water in the steel cools the plastic until it is stiff.

11
00:00:38.717 --> 00:00:43.247
Then the mould opens and the part drops out, here onto a scale.

12
00:00:43.798 --> 00:00:48.221
How hot, how fast and with how much pressure: it is all on one sheet.

13
00:00:49.421 --> 00:00:54.204
That is the setting sheet: the approved settings for one mould on one press.

14
00:00:54.754 --> 00:00:57.180
The setter always starts from this sheet.

15
00:00:58.080 --> 00:01:03.490
Heater zones hold the melt temperature: for PP about 230 degrees.

16
00:01:04.040 --> 00:01:06.141
The mould is at 40 degrees.

17
00:01:06.692 --> 00:01:09.757
Too cold, and the plastic freezes too early.

18
00:01:10.307 --> 00:01:12.768
Too hot, and it needs longer cooling.

19
00:01:13.968 --> 00:01:19.494
The screw pushes forward at the injection speed: here 80 millimetres per second.

20
00:01:20.044 --> 00:01:23.994
Filling fast keeps the plastic hot, so it flows far.

21
00:01:24.544 --> 00:01:26.529
Too fast gives burn marks.

22
00:01:27.429 --> 00:01:32.624
The flow front runs from the gate, the narrow entrance, through the cavity.

23
00:01:33.174 --> 00:01:36.297
Against the cooler steel a thin skin freezes.

24
00:01:37.197 --> 00:01:43.896
From 60 to 12 millimetres is 48 millimetres on speed: at 80 millimetres per second,

25
00:01:44.096 --> 00:01:46.290
0.6 seconds of filling.

26
00:01:47.490 --> 00:01:50.356
At 12 millimetres lies the switchover point:

27
00:01:50.356 --> 00:01:53.156
the machine changes from speed to pressure.

28
00:01:54.056 --> 00:01:58.178
The cavity is then about 95 to 98 percent full.

29
00:01:58.728 --> 00:02:03.488
Switching too late gives flash: a thin film of plastic on the parting line.

30
00:02:04.688 --> 00:02:08.765
Now the holding pressure takes over, here 400 bar.

31
00:02:09.316 --> 00:02:12.404
The screw keeps pushing and packs in more plastic,

32
00:02:12.604 --> 00:02:15.065
because the part shrinks as it cools.

33
00:02:15.965 --> 00:02:20.528
The holding time lasts until the gate has frozen: here 4 seconds.

34
00:02:21.078 --> 00:02:23.284
After that nothing more can get in.

35
00:02:24.184 --> 00:02:28.599
Then 10 seconds of cooling, and about 4 seconds to open,

36
00:02:28.798 --> 00:02:32.827
eject and close: a cycle of 18.6 seconds.

37
00:02:34.027 --> 00:02:39.228
During cooling the screw doses: it turns and moves back against the back pressure.

38
00:02:39.778 --> 00:02:41.160
That mixes the melt.

39
00:02:42.060 --> 00:02:46.033
On the screw tip sits the check ring, a small valve ring.

40
00:02:46.583 --> 00:02:51.683
During injection it closes, so the melt cannot flow back past the screw.

41
00:02:52.583 --> 00:02:56.809
In front of the screw a cushion remains: a few millimetres of melt.

42
00:02:57.359 --> 00:03:01.587
If it jumps from 2 to 8 millimetres, the ring is leaking.

43
00:03:02.787 --> 00:03:06.282
The process window is the area where every part is good.

44
00:03:06.832 --> 00:03:11.536
Sit in the middle, not on the edge: then the process absorbs swings.

45
00:03:12.436 --> 00:03:17.941
Scientific moulding sets the process with measurements, instead of trial and error.

46
00:03:19.141 --> 00:03:23.158
The viscosity curve shows how thick the melt is at each speed.

47
00:03:23.708 --> 00:03:28.146
First it falls 31 percent, then only 6.

48
00:03:28.696 --> 00:03:33.075
Choose the speed where it runs flat: there, swings matter little.

49
00:03:33.975 --> 00:03:38.746
The intensification ratio converts oil pressure into plastic pressure:

50
00:03:38.746 --> 00:03:39.768
often about 10.

51
00:03:40.668 --> 00:03:45.254
A pressure drop study measures the pressure per stretch of the flow path: nozzle,

52
00:03:45.454 --> 00:03:47.323
runner, gate and part.

53
00:03:47.873 --> 00:03:51.530
Together 1,500 of the 2,000 bar.

54
00:03:52.430 --> 00:03:57.320
That is 75 percent, under the rule of thumb of 80 percent.

55
00:03:57.870 --> 00:04:01.713
So not pressure-limited: the press can still reach the set speed.

56
00:04:02.613 --> 00:04:07.025
The gate freeze study weighs parts at ever longer holding times.

57
00:04:07.925 --> 00:04:11.446
Once the weight levels off, the gate is shut.

58
00:04:11.995 --> 00:04:16.990
Set the holding time just above that, often by half a second to a second.

59
00:04:18.190 --> 00:04:23.684
Validation proves that the process always makes good parts, in three steps,

60
00:04:23.884 --> 00:04:29.773
IQ/OQ/PQ: installation, the edges of the window, and production.

61
00:04:30.673 --> 00:04:34.899
After that the part weight watches the process: if the filling changes,

62
00:04:35.099 --> 00:04:36.585
the weight changes.

63
00:04:37.135 --> 00:04:44.341
The control limits lie three standard deviations either side of the average:

64
00:04:44.341 --> 00:04:46.806
here 4.802 to 4.838 grams.

65
00:04:47.356 --> 00:04:52.969
If a part falls outside, something has changed, before parts go bad.

66
00:04:53.869 --> 00:04:59.050
If the mould moves to another press, or a new material supplier comes in,

67
00:04:59.250 --> 00:05:01.073
revalidation follows.
